  "source_definitions": {
    "Di": "distance on the optical axis between the i-th and (i+1)-th surface in mm",
    "Ndi": "refractive index at d-line, wavelength 587.6 nm",
    "vdi": "Abbe number at d-line",
    "published_field": "2? is the full field angle",
    "published_image_height_mm": 2.35,
    "asphere_formula_paragraph": "(76) The imaging lens assembly 21 used in the following examples includes lenses having aspheric surfaces. The aspheric shape of the lens is defined by the following formula (9): Z=C×h 2/{1+(1 -K×C 2 ×h 2)1⁄2 }+ΣAn×h n (9)",
    "asphere_order_paragraph": "(77) (n=an integer greater than or equal to 3).",
    "asphere_symbol_paragraph": "(78) In the formula (9), Z is a depth of the aspheric surface, C is a paraxial curvature which is equal to 1/R, h is a distance from the optical axis to a lens surface, K is a conic constant (second-order aspheric coefficient), and An is an nth-order aspheric coefficient. First Example"
  },
  "exact_transformations": {
    "half_field": "published 2? divided by 2",
    "conic": "K_codev = K_source - 1",
    "terminal_image": "published Image Plane row becomes the planar optical-filter exit carrying the final air gap; a zero-thickness image surface is appended",
    "folded_path": "published Di distances retained along an unfolded scalar sequential path; mirrors remain material-free planes"
  },
